    symptom: "New NVDIMM detected and has been disabled.",
    cause: "The identified NVDIMM is a new NVDIMM and must be sanitized before use.",
    action: "Sanitize the NVDIMM to enable the NVDIMM.\n\n**CAUTION**: Failure to properly handle DIMMs can damage the DIMM components and the system board connector. For more information, see the DIMM handling guidelines in the troubleshooting guide for your product on the Hewlett Packard Enterprise website:\n\nHPE ProLiant Gen10 (http://www.hpe.com/info/gen10-troubleshooting)\nHPE Synergy (http://www.hpe.com/info/synergy-troubleshootings)\n\n1. From the **System Utilities** screen, select **System Configuration > BIOS/Platform Configuration (RBSU) > System Options > Memory Operations > NVDIMM Memory Options**.\n2. Select **NVDIMM-N Sanitize/Erase on Next Reboot Policy**.\n\nThe following alert is displayed.\n\n**IMPORTANT**: Sanitizing/Erasing an NVDIMM-N will result in the loss of ALL user data saved in the NVDIMM-N. Hewlett Packard Enterprise recommends that manual backup of all user data in the NVDIMM-Ns be performed before sanitizing/erasing the NVDIMM-Ns.\n\n3. Press any key to proceed.\n4. Select one of the following menu items: \n**Sanitize/Erase and Boot to Operating System**\n**Sanitize/Erase and Power System Off**\n**Sanitize/Erase and Boot to System Utilities**\n\n5. Select one of the following menu items based on the NVDIMM-Ns to be sanitized/erased: \n**Sanitize/Erase all NVDIMM-N in the System**—Use this option for major system reconfigurations.\n**Sanitize/Erase all NVDIMM-N on Processor 1**—Use this option when all the NVDIMM-Ns on a processor must be sanitized together, such as when enabling NVDIMM-N Memory Interleaving.\n**Sanitize/Erase Processor 1 DIMM X**—This option is available only when NVDIMM-N Memory Interleaving is disabled. Use this option for adding individual NVDIMM-Ns, or for using an NVDIMM-N again after errors occur.\n**Sanitize/Erase all NVDIMM-N on Processor 2**—Use this option when all the NVDIMM-Ns on a processor must be sanitized together, such as when enabling NVDIMM-N Memory Interleaving.\n**Sanitize/Erase Processor 2 DIMM X**—This option is available only when NVDIMM-N Memory Interleaving is disabled. Use this option for adding individual NVDIMM-Ns, or for using an NVDIMM-N again after errors occur.\n\nWhen enabled, all user data in the selected NVDIMM-N is erased on the next reboot. The Sanitize NVDIMM-N scope selections are available only after a sanitize policy is selected.\n\n6. Press the F10 key to save the changes.\n7. Reboot the server.\n\nThe server will reboot multiple times as part of the sanitize process. POST messages will also display to indicate the NVDIMM-Ns that are being sanitized.\n\nIf the issues persists, replace the NVDIMM.\n\n1. Verify the component is either under warranty or contract. Go to \nhttps://support.hpe.com/hpesc/public/home, and then click **Warranty Status**.\n\nThis enables HPE to ship replacement parts directly to you once a failure has been confirmed. For specific information about customer replaceable parts, refer to the product maintenance and service guide.\n\n2. Under **Support Cases**, click **Access Cases**, and then create a support case. \nHPE Support will contact you.\n\n3. When you receive the replacement part, remove all power. \n 1. Disconnect each power cord from the power source.\n 2. Disconnect each power cord from the server\n\n4. Remove the DIMM. \n**CAUTION**: Failure to properly handle DIMMs can cause damage to DIMM components and the system board connector. See the DIMM handling guidelines on the Hewlett Packard Enterprise Library at: http://www.hpe.com/info/enterprise/docs\n\n5. Install the new DIMM.",
