    action: "1. Reseat the DIMMs. \nFor more information, see the server maintenance and service guide on the Hewlett Packard Enterprise website (http://www.hpe.com/info/enterprise/docs).\n\n2. If the issue persists, reseat the processors. \n**CAUTION:** Before removing or replacing any processors, be sure to follow the processor troubleshooting guidelines in \"Performing Processor Procedures in the Troubleshooting Process\" in the HPE ProLiant Troubleshooting Guide, Volume I: Troubleshooting. Failure to follow the recommended guidelines can cause damage to the system board, requiring replacement of the system board.\n\n3. If the issue persists, contact Hewlett Packard Enterprise support.\n\n**Reseating DIMMs**\n\n1. At the next available maintenance window power down your server.\n2. Remove all power. \n 1. Disconnect each power cord from the power source.\n 2. Disconnect each power cord from the server.\n\n3. Remove the DIMM. \n**CAUTION:** Failure to properly handle DIMMs can cause damage to DIMM components and the system board connector. See the DIMM handling guidelines on the Hewlett Packard Enterprise Library at: http://www.hpe.com/info/enterprise/docs\n\n4. Clean the DIMM and the DIMM slot with anti-static compressed air.\n5. Reinstall the DIMM.\n\n**Reseating the processors**\n\n**WARNING**: To reduce the risk of personal injury from hot surfaces, allow the drives and the internal system components to cool before touching them.\n\n**CAUTION**: THE PINS ON THE SYSTEM BOARD ARE VERY FRAGILE AND EASILY DAMAGED. To avoid damage to the system board, do not touch the processor or the processor socket contacts.\n\n**IMPORTANT**: Both processor sockets must always be populated. If either processor socket is empty, the server does not power up.",
