    title: "HPE Persistent Memory Initialization Error: Processor %1 DIMM %2 (SN: %3)",
    symptom: "HPE Persistent Memory Initialization Error: Processor %1 DIMM %2 (SN: %3). The PMM media is not available for use. The PMM controller is still operational.",
    cause: "The system ROM failed to train the Persistent Memory Module (PMM), and thus the media device has been disabled. The module's controller is still enabled.",
    action: "1. Review documentation for more information on supported configurations.\nFor more information, see the HPE Persistent Memory User Guide for HPE ProLiant Gen10 servers and HPE Synergy.\n\n2. If the issue persists, check if there is a newer version of the firmware available for the PMM controller and the system ROM, otherwise replace the PMM.\n\nTo update firmware:\n\nService Pack for ProLiant (SPP) contains server drivers, systems software, and firmware. For more information about SPP, see the SPP website (http://www.hpe.com/servers/spp). Each SPP has an accompanying server support guide that lists the supported servers and components available for each of the supported servers. The server support guides and Service Pack for ProLiant Quick Start Guide are available on the SPP Information Library (http://www.hpe.com/info/spp/documentation).\n\nTo update firmware on HPE Apollo and HPE ProLiant products, see the Smart Update Manager User Guide and the SUM Best Practices Planning Guide at http://www.hpe.com/info/SmartUpdate/docs. The SPP uses Smart Update Manager (SUM) to deploy firmware and system software components.\n\nTo update the firmware on HPE Synergy products, see the Best Practices for HPE Synergy Firmware and Driver Updates at http://www.hpe.com/info/synergy-docs..\n\n3. If the issue still presists, replace the Persistent Memory Module. \n 1. Remove all power. \n 1. Disconnect each power cord from the power source.\n 2. Disconnect each power cord from the server.\n\n 2. Remove the Persistent Memory Module.\n**CAUTION:** Failure to properly handle DIMMs can cause damage to DIMM components and the system board connector. See the DIMM handling guidelines on the Hewlett Packard Enterprise Library at http:// www.hpe.com/info/enterprise/docs.\n\n 3. Install the new Persistent Memory Module.\n\n1. Verify the component is either under warranty or contract. Go to \nhttps://support.hpe.com/hpesc/public/home, and then click **Check Status** under the **Warranty Status** heading.\n\nThis enables HPE to ship replacement parts directly to you once a failure has been confirmed. For specific information about customer replaceable parts, refer to the product maintenance and service guide.\n\n2. Under **Support Cases**, click **Access Cases**, and then create a support case. \nHPE Support will contact you.",
